#!/usr/bin/env beg

Simulate a computer with a filesystem and a process table in memory. Both
start empty. Use stdin, stdout, and stderr. All commands operate on the
simulated state.

Read UTF-8 commands one line at a time. Separate the command and its arguments
with spaces. A path or process name is one word. Print no prompt.

- "write PATH TEXT" creates or replaces a file with TEXT, which extends to the
  end of the line. Print "OK" and a newline.
- "cat PATH" prints the file contents followed by a newline.
- "start NAME" creates a simulated process and prints its decimal PID and a
  newline. Assign consecutive PIDs starting at 1.
- "ps" prints one PID, a tab, and its name per line, in increasing PID order.
- "kill PID" removes that process. Print "OK" and a newline.
- "exit CODE" ends the simulation with the given decimal status from 0 to 255.

For a missing file, print "error: file not found" and a newline to stderr.
For an unknown PID, print "error: process not found" and a newline to stderr.
For a malformed command, print "error: invalid command" and a newline to
stderr. Continue after these errors. At EOF process any final unterminated
line, then exit 0 if the session is still running.
